This is the election-day manual. The constitution lays down the principles (sections 83 to 105); the organic act fills in:

- candidacy, campaigning, spending caps, and what is banned, such as giving property to induce a vote;
- out-of-district, advance and overseas voting, as section 95 allows;
- counting, tallying and announcing results; the Election Commission must announce results for at least 95% of districts within 60 days (section 85);
- the list-seat formula, which since the 2022 amendment divides the nationwide party-ballot total by 100 to get votes per seat, then shares seats in proportion, with largest remainders filling any left over;
- how seats are adjusted when some districts re-vote before results are announced or within one year (sections 93 and 94).

Notice: many rules that change real election outcomes sit in the organic act, not in the constitution. Switching the divisor from 500 to 100 did not require changing a single word of the constitution.

The NACC has nine members serving a single seven-year term (sections 232 and 233). Its work has three strands (section 234):
- investigating politicians, Constitutional Court judges, members of independent organs and the Auditor-General for unusual wealth, corruption, wilful abuse of power against the constitution or law, or serious breaches of ethical standards;
- investigating state officials for corruption and unusual wealth;
- requiring declarations of assets and liabilities for officials, their spouses and minor children, checking them and publishing the results.

The route for cases against politicians (section 235): the NACC must decide by at least half of all its existing members, then the case splits two ways:
- a serious ethical breach → the Supreme Court;
- criminal and other cases → the Attorney-General, who prosecutes before the Supreme Court's Criminal Division for Persons Holding Political Positions.
Once a court accepts the case, the accused stops performing duties unless the court orders otherwise. If found liable, the person is out of office from the date of suspension and must lose the right to stand for election, which bars them for life from standing for any election or holding any political office; the court may also revoke their right to vote for up to ten years. Removal on this ground is also a disqualification under section 98(18).

Who checks the NACC (section 236)? MPs, senators or members of both houses numbering at least one-fifth of the total membership of both houses, or 20,000 voters, may file an accusation against an NACC member with the President of the National Assembly. If there is reasonable doubt, the President sends it to the President of the Supreme Court, who appoints an independent inquiry panel. This is the constitution's answer to the old question: who watches the watchmen?

The National Human Rights Commission has seven members with knowledge and experience in protecting rights and liberties, and political neutrality (section 246), serving a single seven-year term under the template.

Duties (section 247):
- investigating and reporting every human-rights violation without delay, and recommending remedies and redress to state or private bodies;
- producing a national human-rights assessment for Parliament and the Cabinet, and publishing it;
- recommending legal reform in line with human-rights principles;
- clarifying the facts when reports on human rights in Thailand are inaccurate or unfair;
- raising awareness of human rights.
On receiving reports or recommendations, the Cabinet must act promptly or explain why it cannot.

A point of debate: the duty in section 247(4) to 'clarify' inaccurate reports has been criticised by some human-rights organisations as risking turning the Commission into the government's defender, while the drafters saw it as providing accurate information to the international community. The Commission's international credibility depends on how it uses that duty.

Note the difference from the Ombudsmen: the Ombudsmen deal with unfair administration, the NHRC with human-rights violations, and when the two overlap the Ombudsmen pass the matter to the NHRC (section 230).

The constitution builds the frame: nine judges (section 200), a single seven-year term (section 207), and core powers to review laws and bills and settle disputes over the powers of constitutional bodies (section 210). A panel needs at least seven judges and decides by majority; no judge may refuse to decide by claiming the matter is outside the Court's jurisdiction; and rulings are final and bind every state body (section 211).

The organic act fills in:
- who may file, through which channel, and on what conditions;
- direct petitions by people whose constitutional rights are violated under section 213, subject to the time limits and conditions in the act;
- an inquisitorial procedure: the Court can seek facts itself instead of relying only on what the parties present;
- interim measures before a ruling, and suspensions from duty where the constitution allows;
- powers to keep order and punish contempt, separating good-faith academic criticism of rulings from criticism using rude, sarcastic or threatening language;
- the selection of judges under section 203.

The logic: a court whose rulings are final, with no higher court to appeal to, needs especially clear and predictable procedure, because procedure is the only thing the losing side can accept.
